  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA tranquil stretch of October weather is upon us the next few \ndays. Responsible for the overall quiet weather is our regions \nplacement in a dry, northwesterly flow pattern aloft. The only \nweather today to note is a warm front that is lifting northward.\nThe area of strongest WAA can be approximated on satellite by \nthe shield of developing cloud cover over southeastern ND into \nwest central MN. This lift should be enough to generate a couple\nof showers later this afternoon and evening as it tracks \nthrough west central MN. There is meager instability (MUCAPE of \nup to 500 J/Kg), which may produce a stray strike or two of \nlightning with the strongest showers. However, dry air near the \nsurface will limit most locations from meaningful rainfall. \n\nMidweek, a wave passes to our north and east. While no \nprecipitation is forecasted in this FA, it will bring a period \nof cooler but still above average temperatures (more upper 60s \nand low 70s to end the week versus widespread 70s on \nMonday/Tuesday). \n\nThereafter, upper flow become more amplified, with troughing \nover the western CONUS, and ridging downstream over the \ncentral/eastern US. This will set our region up into some \nvariation of a southwest/split flow by the very end of the \nweekend into the first part of next week. While model guidance \nis picking up on this more active pattern, there are quite a few\ndisagreements in the details, ranging from how many waves are \nejected into the region, the timing of each, and their strength.\nWhile this uncertainty keeps precipitation specifics murky, \nthere is agreement within model guidance that after a brief \nwarmup with widespread 70s on Saturday, we will cool down \nsubstantially by next Monday. High temperatures by Monday are \nforecasted to be on either side of 50 degrees.",
